[06/07 12:00:25    743s] Usage: dbGet [-help] [-d] [-u] [-v] [-e] [-i <num>] [-regexp] [-p[<num>]] 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]              {<obj>|<objList>|head|top|selected}[.<objType>]...[[.<attrName>|.?|.??|.?h] [<pattern>] 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]              | [expression]]
[06/07 12:00:25    743s] 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s] -help       # Prints out the command usage 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s] -d          # User specified values and return values are in database units.
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # Default: All values are in user units, in microns. (bool, optional) 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s] -u          # Removes duplicate objects from the query results so that the 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # results list contains only unique entries. (bool, optional) 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s] -v          # Return values will be items that do not match the specified 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # <pattern> or <expression> (bool, optional) 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # Example: dbGet -v -p2 top.nets.sWires.layer.name M4 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             #    returns list of pointers to sWire objects on layers other than M4 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s] -e          # For avoiding NULL (0x0) pointers and results from being printed. (bool, optional) 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # This would effect all kinds of outputs. In cases of nested dbGet statements 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # -e option should not be used in the inner dbGet statement. This is an incorrect usage 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # Example : CORRECT USAGE : 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # dbGet -e [dbGet -p2 [dbGet -p top.insts.pStatus fixed].cell.name INV1] 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # If none of the instances have 'fixed' placement status, the outer dbGet 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # will not return a 0x0 but an empty list which TCL would not consider as a list element 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # INCORRECT USAGE : 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # dbGet [dbGet -p2 [dbGet -p -e top.insts.pStatus fixed].cell.name INV1] 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s] -i <num>    # For selecting a specific element from the list being returned as output 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # -i <num> would select the the <num>th element starting from 0 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # -i <num> option would also reduce a starting '{' and ending '}' for box and pt type attributes 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # Example : dbGet top.nets.name sum* -p -i 0 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # would return 1st pointer in the list of the nets whose name matches the pattern sum* 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s] -regexp     # Uses regular expression <pattern> matching (equivalent to Tcl regexp 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # command) for string/enum attributes. The option does not apply to 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # filtering done using the <expression> method, where the Tcl regexp 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # command can be used directly.
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # Default: Uses simple wild card pattern matching (bool, optional) 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # Example: dbGet -regexp -p top.insts.name M.* 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             #    returns list of pointers to instances whose name starts with M 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s] -p[<num>]   # Specifies the number of levels to traverse back through the 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # specified objects for the query. (-p = 1 level, num indicates 1 
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # or more) (bool, optional) The -p option is used only with either a
[06/07 12:00:25    743s]             # <pattern> or an <expression> to filter out objects.
